    title: "Yemen: Warring sides exchange shelling and air strikes, as Saudi oil facilities burn",
    summary: "Yemen: Warring sides exchange shelling and air strikes, as Saudi oil facilities burn Submitted by MEE staff on Thu, 11/26/2026 - 10:19 Saudi-led coalition warplanes have targeted the capital Sanaa, while shelling by Houthi aligned forces killed a woman in Taiz A satellite image shows large plumes of smoke billowing over Saudi Ras Tanura oil refinery on Wednesday, after Yemen's Houthi military leadership said in a statement that they attacked the industrial facility (Reuters) Off Houthi shelling southeast of Yemen's Taiz has killed a woman and wounded at least seven others, including a child, the government-run Saba news agency reported. The news agency reported that the shelling targeted villages in the Sabir al-Mawadim and al-Misrakh districts of Jabal Sabir. Several homes were also damaged in the attacks, while the injured were taken to hospital for treatment. A series of air strikes by the Saudi-led coalition also targeted the capital Sanaa, according to media affiliated with the Houthi-led government. An explosion was reported earlier in the city on Thursday following recent air strikes near the airport. Forces loyal to the internationally recognised government also said they carried out air strikes on Houthi positions in the Al-Aqroud area of Al-Misrakh district, south of Taiz, following the attacks. On Wednesday, the Houthis claimed attacks on Saudi Arabia’s Red Sea ports of Jeddah and Jazan, as the kingdom’s deterrence and defensive capabilities continue to be challenged. Riyadh is shooting down the majority of Houthi missiles and drones fired at it, Arab and western officials told Middle East Eye, but it has failed to protect key assets like energy facilities, financial centres and airports. Houthis say they attacked Saudi Arabia's largest oil refinery in retaliation for deadly strikes S and regional states to his side but with mixed success. The US has been reluctant to engage Houthi-aligned forces after its previous failed bombing campaign against them was ended last year. Egypt, Pakistan, Turkey and Syria have all sent or committed to deliver military support to Saudi Arabia but - with the exception of Syria, which has sent drone operators - the assistance is defensive. The Saudi crown prince met US Central Command chief Admiral Brad Cooper, the commander of US forces in the Middle East, last week. MEE first reported the planned meeting, which was the third since fighting with the Houthis resumed in July. The US, Saudi Arabia's top military supplier, has not publicly committed additional support to the kingdom, although it has troops and advisers inside the country. Semafor reported on Wednesday that Mohammed bin Salman will travel to the US next month for the G20 summit in Miami. Diplomats have speculated for months about whether the de facto leader of Saudi Arabia would leave the kingdom amid Houthi strikes and the chances of a new US-Israeli attack on Iran. Yemen War News Post Date Override 0 Update Date Mon, 05/04/2020 - 21:19 Update Date Override 0",
    preview: "Yemen: Warring sides exchange shelling and air strikes, as Saudi oil facilities burn Submitted by MEE staff on Thu, 11/26/2026 - 10:19 Saudi-led coalition warplanes have targeted the capital Sanaa, while shelling by Houthi aligned forces killed a woman in Taiz A satellite image shows large plumes of smoke billowing over Saudi Ras Tanura oil refinery on Wednesday, after Yemen's Houthi military leadership said in a statement that they attacked the industrial facility (Reuters) Off Houthi shelling southeast of Yemen's Taiz has killed a woman and wounded at…",
    body: "Yemen: Warring sides exchange shelling and air strikes, as Saudi oil facilities burn Submitted by MEE staff on Thu, 11/26/2026 - 10:19 Saudi-led coalition warplanes have targeted the capital Sanaa, while shelling by Houthi aligned forces killed a woman in Taiz A satellite image shows large plumes of smoke billowing over Saudi Ras Tanura oil refinery on Wednesday, after Yemen's Houthi military leadership said in a statement that they attacked the industrial facility (Reuters) Off Houthi shelling southeast of Yemen's Taiz has killed a woman and wounded at least seven others, including a child, the government-run Saba news agency reported.\n\nThe news agency reported that the shelling targeted villages in the Sabir al-Mawadim and al-Misrakh districts of Jabal Sabir. Several homes were also damaged in the attacks, while the injured were taken to hospital for treatment. A series of air strikes by the Saudi-led coalition also targeted the capital Sanaa, according to media affiliated with the Houthi-led government. An explosion was reported earlier in the city on Thursday following recent air strikes near the airport.\n\nForces loyal to the internationally recognised government also said they carried out air strikes on Houthi positions in the Al-Aqroud area of Al-Misrakh district, south of Taiz, following the attacks. On Wednesday, the Houthis claimed attacks on Saudi Arabia’s Red Sea ports of Jeddah and Jazan, as the kingdom’s deterrence and defensive capabilities continue to be challenged. Riyadh is shooting down the majority of Houthi missiles and drones fired at it, Arab and western officials told Middle East Eye, but it has failed to protect key assets like energy facilities, financial centres and airports.\n\nHouthis say they attacked Saudi Arabia's largest oil refinery in retaliation for deadly strikes S and regional states to his side but with mixed success. The US has been reluctant to engage Houthi-aligned forces after its previous failed bombing campaign against them was ended last year. Egypt, Pakistan, Turkey and Syria have all sent or committed to deliver military support to Saudi Arabia but - with the exception of Syria, which has sent drone operators - the assistance is defensive.\n\nThe Saudi crown prince met US Central Command chief Admiral Brad Cooper, the commander of US forces in the Middle East, last week. MEE first reported the planned meeting, which was the third since fighting with the Houthis resumed in July. The US, Saudi Arabia's top military supplier, has not publicly committed additional support to the kingdom, although it has troops and advisers inside the country.\n\nSemafor reported on Wednesday that Mohammed bin Salman will travel to the US next month for the G20 summit in Miami. Diplomats have speculated for months about whether the de facto leader of Saudi Arabia would leave the kingdom amid Houthi strikes and the chances of a new US-Israeli attack on Iran. Yemen War News Post Date Override 0 Update Date Mon, 05/04/2020 - 21:19 Update Date Override 0",
