    cat: "economia-tech",
    categories: ["economia-tech", "geopolitica"],
    title: "US announces new tariffs on host of countries including China",
    summary: "The new levies would hit many of America's biggest trading partners, including China and the EU.",
    preview: "The US has announced a fresh raft of tariffs as President Donald Trump pushes ahead with his signature trade policy. The new taxes charged on imports to America are set to hit many of the country's biggest trading partners - China, the European Union and 12 other jurisdictions, including Japan, South Korea and Taiwan. The White House began investigating the trade practices of other countries in March after the US Supreme Court ruled Trump's previously announced Liberation Day tariffs were illegal.",
    body: "The US has announced a fresh raft of tariffs as President Donald Trump pushes ahead with his signature trade policy.\n\nThe new taxes charged on imports to America are set to hit many of the country's biggest trading partners - China, the European Union and 12 other jurisdictions, including Japan, South Korea and Taiwan.\n\nThe White House began investigating the trade practices of other countries in March after the US Supreme Court ruled Trump's previously announced Liberation Day tariffs were illegal.\n\nChina is facing the highest tariff rate of up to 40%, followed by Vietnam at up to 35%, with others facing either 20% or 25%.\n\nThe US Trade Representative (USTR) announced the proposed tariffs together with extensive reports of how each jurisdiction's trade practices purportedly impact American commerce.\n\nThe new duties are not expected to take effect until next year and have to go through rounds of public consultation and, potentially, hearings.\n\nTariffs are taxes levied on imported goods. The amount is typically calculated as a percentage of a good's value and paid to the government by businesses importing products.\n\nSome of these rates will be effectively reduced because of caps on tariffs Trump has agreed to. Individual countries also face different levies.\n\nThe proposed tariffs will affect goods from China, the European Union, Singapore, Switzerland, Indonesia, Malaysia, Cambodia, Thailand, Korea, Vietnam, Taiwan, Bangladesh, Japan, and India.\n\nThe USTR's Jamieson Greer said the latest tariffs issued would \"level the playing field for American workers and chart a new, more sustainable course for the world economy\".\n\nHe said that many of America's trading partners had \"for decades\" adopted industrial strategies which included \"targeting manufacturing, wage and labor suppression\".\n\nThey also employ \"other market distorting practices that tilt the trade balance in their favour while eroding America's manufacturing base\", Greer said.\n\nBut economists have warned tariffs can fuel inflation and push prices up for consumers if businesses pass on higher costs, as well as dampen global trade.\n\nTrump has repeatedly threatened or imposed tariffs during his second term, arguing they will boost US manufacturing and create jobs.\n\nDespite a poor result for the Republican party in the midterm elections, the president has continued to press on with his flagship trade policy.\n\nTrump is also seeking to cut the US trade deficit - the gap between the value of goods it buys from other countries and those it sells to them.\n\nThese new levies are build on a \"stronger legal foundation\" under US law compared with Trump's April 2025 Liberation Day tariffs, Singapore Management University economics lecturer Chang Pao-Li said.\n\nTrump previously invoked emergency presidential powers to impose tariffs that have since been struck down, while his latest approach relies on the USTR's authority to impose tariffs to address certain foreign trade practices, she said.\n\nThe levies are still likely to face legal challenges, but there has been a \"historical precedent\" for such tariffs remaining in force, Chang said.\n\nMany tariffs imposed under Section 301 - a law that allows the US to probe and retaliate against foreign trade practices - during Trump's first term remained in effect during his successor Joe Biden's administration.\n\nHowever, Stephen Brown, chief North America economist for Capital Economics, said the latest tariff announcement was \"not as alarming as it looks\", due to the taxes \"essentially replacing\" previously announced tariffs made in the summer.\n\n\"The increase in the effective tariff rate looks set to be a touch lower than we've been assuming for our forecasts, although the new tariffs will still provide a marginal boost to prices,\" he said in a note.\n\nThe additional tariff burden is likely to be small because a limited number of goods are subject to the new duties, Brown added.\n\nJurisdictions affected by the new wave of tariffs have voiced their disapproval over the Trump administration's move.\n\nWashington has also proposed a 10% tariff on goods from Brazil, as part of a separate investigation over what it says are \"harmful trade practices\" undercutting American workers.\n\nBrazilian authorities expressed their \"outrage\" at the announcement, saying that the move is aimed at pressuring the government to change regulations around issues like digital trade.\n\nThe South American nation faces duties on finished aircraft and sugar sold to the US.\n\n\"Brazil will not yield to such threats,\" President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva's office said.\n\nThe European Commission rejected the new US tariffs, saying it would investigate the reasons for bringing them in. It pointed out that tariffs are limited to the 15% previously agreed.\n\nThe latest tariff rate is \"not consistent\" with the reality of bilateral economic-trade cooperation between the US and Vietnam, Vietnamese Deputy Prime Minister Bui Thanh Son said.\n\nThe Japanese government said the US tariffs were \"regrettable\", while the Singapore government said it disagrees with the White House's findings and that it is engaging the USTR to explore ways to reduce the tariff.\n\nSouth Korea's trade minister said the proposed 15% tariff will be \"more than a decent outcome\" as it would fall in line with a previously agreed limit with Washington.\n\nLast year, Seoul pledged $350bn (£267bn) in US investments in exchange for lower tariffs as part of a trade deal.\n\n- Trump tariffs on Asia: Which economies were hit hardest?\n\n- Published1 August 2025\n\n- China's growth accelerates as AI boom helps boost exports\n\n- Published19 October\n\nUS trading partners have been \"waiting for this shoe to drop\" and have argued that they are being wrongly accused of producing too much, said the Asia Society Policy Institute's Wendy Cutler, a former senior US trade negotiator.\n\n\"Many believe that China, not them, should be the target for US action,\" she added.\n\nChinese Foreign Ministry spokesperson Guo Jiakun described the new tariffs as a form of \"political manipulation\", describing as \"false\" the US claim that China produces more than it can consume.\n\nThe tariff announcement is likely to increase tensions between the US and China, the world's two biggest economies, who remain locked in a trade dispute and the AI race.\n\nThe two countries have also been embroiled in a tit-for-tat tariffs war, which is currently on hold.\n\nDespite last week's meeting between Trump and China's President Xi Jinping, the two sides made no announcement on the trade war truce, which is set to expire on 10 January.\n\nBeijing has remained vague on how it will respond but for now seems \"prepared to live with the new rates provided they don't exceed 20%\", Cutler said.\n\nAdditional\n\n- Published22 August",
